#!/usr/bin/python3 -sE
# duo2-wwan: the modem's switch and mobile data without NetworkManager
# (telephony/README.md). duo2-os runs systemd-networkd + wpa_supplicant for
# WiFi; this small daemon does for the modem what NetworkManager would:
#
# - a modem whose SIM is ready (ModemManager: 'disabled') is enabled once when
#   it appears: online, registered, so calls and SMS reach Calls and Chatty. A
#   later `mmcli -m any --disable` is respected until the modem appears anew
#   (ModemManager restart, modem restart, SIM change);
# - no primary subscription although a card with a USIM is in a slot
#   ('sim-missing'): `duo2-sim auto` picks one (physical slot 1 first); a card
#   put in while running is left to the modem's own provisioning for up to
#   30 s, then ModemManager is restarted to look again;
# - registered (at home, or roaming when ROAMING=yes) and DATA=yes: a data
#   bearer (ModemManager's simple connect), and the network device it reports
#   is configured here: address, MTU, a default route with ROUTE_METRIC (above
#   WiFi's 1024, so WiFi stays the default while it is up), DNS through
#   systemd-resolved; a dropped bearer is retried with a growing pause.
#
# - the user's mobile data switch (the shell's quick menu, through
#   duo-shell-helper): /var/lib/duo2/mobile-data, `on` or `off`, watched
#   (a file monitor): off disconnects the bearer at once, on connects;
#   without the file, wwan.conf's DATA decides;
# - airplane mode (the quick menu, duo-shell-helper): /var/lib/duo2/airplane,
#   `on` or `off`, watched the same way: on disables the modem and puts it in
#   low power (RF off; the helper blocks WiFi and Bluetooth itself), off
#   powers it up and enables it again. Kept across reboots (the file);
# - WiFi first: while another device has an IPv4 default route (WiFi), the
#   bearer keeps its addresses and its IPv4 route at ROUTE_METRIC, but has no
#   IPv6 default route and is no DNS default route in resolved: a WiFi
#   without IPv6 would otherwise send every IPv6 connection (most of them,
#   apps prefer IPv6) over the mobile network. Followed on netlink;
# - registered but no packet service for minutes (3GPP reject cause #7, e.g.
#   data provisioned on the SIM after it attached): the modem keeps that until
#   its radio goes off, so a low-power cycle (disable, low power, enable), at
#   most every 30 min and 3 times per modem appearance, never during a call.
# - smart 5G (SMART_5G=yes): 5G NR (NSA) only while it can matter: mobile
#   data on, a panel lit and the bearer carrying the traffic (WiFi not the
#   default route); otherwise the modem's mode preference without NR. With NR
#   allowed the modem stays in EN-DC and never power-collapses, even with no
#   data at all (measured: asleep 0-23 % with NR, 94 % without; it also gates
#   the DDR's low-power mode in suspend). Our own QMI client sets the NAS mode
#   preference "until the next power cycle", never PERMANENT (what
#   ModemManager's set-allowed-modes sends: a modem NV write); re-applied when
#   the modem appears. NR goes after NR_DENY_DELAY s dark (no flapping on a
#   glance), at once before a suspend (a logind delay inhibitor), and comes
#   back after NR_ALLOW_DELAY s lit; never while a call is dialling, ringing,
#   active or held (deferred until it ends). Devices on the phone's hotspot
#   (hotspot/, its state file says so: a device connected and the user's
#   "use 5G" on) count as data in use: NR allowed with the panels dark and
#   WiFi the phone's own default route too (SMART_5G_HOTSPOT=yes).
# - emergency calls (telephony/README.md "Emergency calls"): ModemManager
#   lets 112 and 911 through in every state (more numbers without a SIM),
#   but it never switches the radio on for one, and this modem waits in low
#   power (duo2-modem-lpm) until somebody does. So with no SIM or a SIM
#   waiting for its PIN (EMERGENCY_RADIO=yes, the default) the radio is put
#   online anyway: the modem camps on any network ("emergency calls only")
#   and 112 goes out at once, as on any phone. And an emergency call dialled
#   while the radio is off (airplane mode, the reject-#7 radio cycle, a
#   modem disabled by hand) switches it on (airplane mode goes off, as on
#   Android); if that first attempt failed for the radio, the same number is
#   dialled once more as soon as the radio is up (never after a hang-up).
#
# - other programs' bearers (VoLTE: the modem's IMS stack asks 81voltd for its
#   own PDNs, `ims`, the emergency `sos`, XCAP's `hos`): only a bearer for the
#   APN duo2-wwan connects with is its data bearer. The others are left alone:
#   never adopted (their link gets no address, route or DNS here), and the
#   mobile data switch disconnects only ours (ModemManager's Simple.Disconnect
#   with no bearer would take every bearer down, IMS registration included).
#
# Event driven (ModemManager's D-Bus signals, netlink, file monitors), no
# polling. Settings: /etc/duo2/wwan.conf, read at start
# (`systemctl restart duo2-wwan`).
